# WeOnline runs standalone — no environment variables are REQUIRED to run locally
# (npm run dev). The billing engine + MikroTik simulator persist to
# data/weonline.json. Copy this file to .env.local (gitignored) if you add config.
#
# ── Live MikroTik routers (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# When you add a router with driver="live", its REST password is stored in
# data/weonline.json (plaintext on disk). To keep the password OUT of that file,
# set an env override per router. The variable name is derived from the router id:
#
#     ROUTER_<ID>_PASSWORD          (id uppercased, non-alphanumerics → "_")
#
# e.g. a router whose id is "rtr_l009_office":
#     ROUTER_RTR_L009_OFFICE_PASSWORD=super-secret
#
# When set, this value is used instead of the stored password for that router.
#
# ── API auth ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The server API (/api/mikrotik, /api/billing) now requires a signed session
# token. AUTH_SECRET signs those tokens — set it to a long random value in
# production (if unset, an ephemeral one is generated and sessions reset on every
# restart). Generate one with:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#     AUTH_SECRET=<64-hex-chars>
#
# Credential fields (router/subscriber passwords) are encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM.
# The key comes from DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY (32 bytes / 64 hex), or is derived from
# AUTH_SECRET if unset. Set it explicitly in production so rotating AUTH_SECRET doesn't
# also change the data key:
#     DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<64-hex-chars>
#
# ── Network binding & TLS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The server binds to 127.0.0.1 by default (API not exposed on the network). To
# serve other devices, put it behind an authenticating reverse proxy / VPN (see
# deploy/) and set:
#     HOST=0.0.0.0
#
# Behind a reverse proxy, set the number of proxy hops so client IPs (and thus
# rate-limiting) are correct. Only set this when actually behind a proxy:
#     TRUST_PROXY=1
#
# Optional native HTTPS (instead of a proxy): point these at a key/cert pair and the
# server serves TLS directly. In most setups a reverse proxy terminates TLS instead.
#     TLS_KEY_FILE=/path/to/privkey.pem
#     TLS_CERT_FILE=/path/to/fullchain.pem
